Found while localising $YAML::XS::Boolean for karr #20e.

File::SOPS::decrypt_file calls YAML::XS::Dump($data) directly rather than asking the format handler to emit. That made it depend on a side effect: Format::YAML set $YAML::XS::Boolean at load time, and without it Dump writes a JSON::PP::Boolean as "!!perl/scalar:JSON::PP::Boolean 1" instead of "true". Localising the variable inside Format::YAML alone would therefore have broken decrypt_file.

Patched for now by localising it in decrypt_file too, from $File::SOPS::Format::YAML::BOOLEAN_MODE so the mode string still has one definition. But the emitter now exists in two places, which is the twin shape this distribution keeps getting bitten by - and the JSON branch of decrypt_file has its own JSON::MaybeXS->new(...) with hand-copied options (utf8/pretty/canonical) that must stay identical to Format::JSON s.

Fix candidate: give both format handlers a plain "emit the data without a sops section" method and have decrypt_file call it. Note the canonical/sorted-key option is MAC-relevant, which is exactly why it should not be duplicated.
Consolidated: new emit() on Format::YAML and Format::JSON emits a document with no sops section; serialize() is emit() plus the metadata section, and File::SOPS::_serialize_plaintext (decrypt_file + edit) is emit() on its own. The JSON encoder options and the YAML boolean-mode local each have exactly ONE definition now.

MAC check first, as asked: the consolidation moves no digest. decrypt_file writes plaintext with no sops section and computes no MAC over it; edit's plaintext only reaches the editor, and the digest of the re-encrypted document is computed by encrypt() over the REPARSED tree via _sorted_leaves (sorted order) regardless of what the editor was shown. Proved byte-neutral rather than argued: fixed-input fingerprints of Format::YAML->serialize, Format::JSON->serialize and _serialize_plaintext for yaml/yml/json, plus the unknown-format croak, are sha256-identical before and after (5/5). Stayed in the format lane; nothing handed to file-sops-wire.

The coupling did not disappear, it moved: emit() is now shared with serialize(), so a future change to what it emits DOES move the wire bytes. Stated in the comments and the POD of both handlers -- the plaintext emitter is no longer a safe place for a cosmetic change.

Regression test t/20-plaintext-emitter.t, 7 subtests / 24 assertions. It does not assert that the two emitters agree (they always did); it replaces emit() and requires decrypt_file, edit and serialize to follow it, then pins the options on that one emit(). Measured red three ways:
  * against HEAD edb7444: 3 of 4 assertions in subtest 1 fail, then the file dies on Can't locate object method 'emit', exit 255, 2/2 subtests failed;
  * against the fix WITH a re-introduced private emitter carrying byte-identical options (the invisible twin): 4 assertions fail, 2 of 7 subtests -- this is the case nothing could catch before;
  * against the fix with the boolean local dropped from YAML emit(): 3 assertions fail. Dropping canonical from the JSON encoder does not even reach the assertion: the round trip dies with a MAC mismatch, which is ADR 0001's coupling firing.

